    <!-- ============================ ADU FEASIBILITY CHECKLIST ============================ -->
    <section class="section">
      <div class="container">
        <div class="section-head center" data-reveal>
          <span class="eyebrow">Before You Commit</span>
          <h2 data-reveal-words>ADU Feasibility Checklist</h2>
          <hr class="rule">
        </div>
        <p class="advisory-lede" data-reveal data-delay="1">Massachusetts' 2024 housing law changed ADU rules statewide, and local implementation is still active. This is a starting checklist of what to verify before assuming a property qualifies, not a permit decision or a guarantee of approval. Reviewed October 2026; rules change, so recheck the primary sources below before relying on any date-sensitive detail.</p>
        <div class="svc-grid">
          <article class="svc-card" data-reveal data-delay="1">
            <div class="svc-card__head">
              <h3>1. State-level status</h3>
            </div>
            <p class="advisory-lede">Massachusetts reported 854 ADU approvals statewide in the first half of 2026, and 2,084 since the start of 2025 &mdash; a statewide figure, not a Falmouth or Cape Cod count. The state's ADU FAQ defines a protected-use size threshold as the lesser of half the principal dwelling's gross floor area or 900 square feet; protected status does not mean exempt from all permitting. <a href="https://www.mass.gov/info-details/accessory-dwelling-units" target="_blank" rel="noopener">State ADU hub</a> and <a href="https://www.mass.gov/info-details/accessory-dwelling-unit-adu-faqs" target="_blank" rel="noopener">state FAQ</a>.</p>
          </article>

          <article class="svc-card" data-reveal data-delay="2">
            <div class="svc-card__head">
              <h3>2. Local zoning implementation</h3>
            </div>
            <p class="advisory-lede">State law sets a floor; local zoning, bylaws and any town-specific ADU regulations still govern setbacks, parking and design review for a given lot. Check Falmouth's current zoning resources directly rather than assuming the state rule applies unchanged. <a href="https://www.falmouthma.gov/1397/Zoning-Signs" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Falmouth zoning resources</a>.</p>
          </article>

          <article class="svc-card" data-reveal data-delay="3">
            <div class="svc-card__head">
              <h3>3. Wastewater and site constraints</h3>
            </div>
            <p class="advisory-lede">Septic capacity, Title 5 requirements, well setbacks, flood zones and conservation restrictions can determine whether a given lot can add a unit at all, independent of zoning. These need a site-specific review by the relevant licensed professional before a design is finalized.</p>
          </article>

          <article class="svc-card" data-reveal data-delay="4">
            <div class="svc-card__head">
              <h3>4. Project economics and timeline</h3>
            </div>
            <p class="advisory-lede">Cost and schedule depend on scope, site conditions and permitting path, and are not estimated here. A written scope and cost discussion follows a site visit and the checks above, not before them. We do not promise a return on investment or a specific permit outcome.</p>
          </article>
        </div>
        <p class="advisory-lede" data-reveal data-delay="5" style="margin-top:24px">This checklist flags what to verify; it is not legal, engineering or permitting advice, and proposed state regulations may change before or after this page was last reviewed. Confirm current rules with the town and a qualified professional for your specific property.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

